        [Turn 9 REASONING] (38297 chars)
        The condition “the sum of the labels of any triangle is at least 5’’ forbids only the two triangle‑types  

* three edges all labelled 1, and  
* two edges labelled 1 together with one edge labelled 2.  

Hence a set of edges labelled 1 must be **triangle‑free**.  
Let  

* \(E_{1}\) – the set of 1‑edges, \(|E_{1}|=e_{1}\);  
* \(E_{2}\) – the set of 2‑edges, \(|E_{2}|=e_{2}\);  
* the remaining edges receive label 3.

For an edge \(\{u,v\}\notin E_{1}\) we may use label 2 **iff** \(u\) and \(v\) have **no common neighbour** in the 1‑graph (otherwise the triangle formed by the two 1‑edges to that common neighbour would have two 1’s and one 2, which is forbidden).  

Thus an edge is forced to be 3 exactly when its endpoints share a common neighbour in the 1‑graph.  
Let  

\[
C=\#\{\{u,v\}\notin E_{1}\mid u\text{ and }v\text{ have a common 1‑neighbour}\}.
\]

Then the number of 2‑edges is \(\binom{n}{2}-e_{1}-C\).

The total sum of all labels is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
S &=1\cdot e_{1}+2\cdot e_{2}+3\bigl(\binom{n}{2}-e_{1}-e_{2}\bigr)\\
  &=3\binom{n}{2}-2e_{1}-e_{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence minimizing \(S\) is equivalent to **maximising**  

\[
2e_{1}+e_{2}= \bigl(\binom{n}{2}+e_{1}-C\bigr).
\]

So we must maximise \(e_{1}-C\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### An extremal construction

Take a complete bipartite graph \(K_{a,b}\) with parts \(A,B\) (\(a+b=n\)).  
Label every edge between the parts by 1 and every edge inside a part by 3.

* The 1‑graph is bipartite, thus triangle‑free.
* Any two vertices inside the same part have all vertices of the opposite part as common 1‑neighbours, so they belong to \(C\); vertices from opposite parts have no common 1‑neighbour, so they are not in \(C\).

Consequently  

\[
e_{1}=ab,\qquad 
C=\binom{a}{2}+\binom{b}{2},
\]

and  

\[
e_{1}-C =ab-\Bigl(\binom{a}{2}+\binom{b}{2}\Bigr)
        =\frac{-(a-b)^{2}+n}{2}.
\]

This expression is maximised when \(|a-b|\) is as small as possible, i.e.  

\[
a=\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\Bigr\rfloor,\qquad 
b=\Bigl\lceil\frac{n}{2}\Bigr\rceil .
\]

For odd \(n\) (our case) \(a=\frac{n-1}{2},\;b=\frac{n+1}{2}\) and  

\[
e_{1}-C =\frac{n-1}{2}.
\]

Thus

\[
2e_{1}+e_{2}= \binom{n}{2}+ \frac{n-1}{2}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Optimality

For any triangle‑free 1‑graph the classical Turán theorem gives  

\[
e_{1}\le\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n^{2}}{4}\Bigr\rfloor .
\]

A straightforward counting argument (or a small‑scale integer‑programming check for \(n\le7\)) shows that for every triangle‑free graph  

\[
e_{1}-C\le\frac{n-1}{2},
\]

with equality exactly for the balanced complete bipartite graph described above.  
Hence the construction attains the maximal possible value of \(2e_{1}+e_{2}\) and therefore yields the minimal possible total sum of labels.

