        Let  

\[
\triangle ABC\qquad (\angle A=30^{\circ},\;\angle B=\angle C =75^{\circ})
\]

and denote  

\[
AB=AC=a ,\qquad BC=b .
\]

From the law of sines  

\[
\frac{a}{\sin 75^{\circ}}=\frac{b}{\sin 30^{\circ}}
      \Longrightarrow 
      \frac{a}{b}= \frac{\sin 75^{\circ}}{\sin 30^{\circ}}
      =\frac{2\sin 75^{\circ}}{1}
      =\frac{\sqrt 2(\sqrt3+1)}{2}\approx1.93185 .
\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Combinatorial restrictions

In a convex polyhedron all faces are congruent to \(\triangle ABC\).

*Each face contributes two edges of length \(a\) (the equal sides) and one
edge of length \(b\) (the base).*

Hence, letting  

\[
E_a,\;E_b \text{ be the numbers of edges of length }a\text{ and }b,
\]

\[
2E_a =2F\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad E_a =F=m,
\]
\[
2E_b =F\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad E_b =\frac{F}{2}= \frac{m}{2}.
\tag{2}
\]

Consequently \(m\) must be even.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### Vertices

Call a vertex **type‑30** if the angle of a face meeting there is the
\(30^{\circ}\) angle, and **type‑75** otherwise.
Let  

\[
A=\#\{\text{type‑30 vertices}\},\qquad
B=\#\{\text{type‑75 vertices}\},\qquad V=A+B .
\]

At a type‑30 vertex \(k\) faces meet, contributing \(30k^{\circ}\) to the
solid angle; convexity forces  

\[
30k<360^{\circ}\Longrightarrow k\le 11 .
\tag{3}
\]

At a type‑75 vertex \(k\) faces meet, so  

\[
75k<360^{\circ}\Longrightarrow k\le 4 .
\tag{4}
\]

Thus each type‑30 vertex has degree \(k\le 11\) and each type‑75 vertex
degree \(k\le 4\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### Degree sums

Every edge of length \(a\) joins a type‑30 vertex to a type‑75 vertex,
hence the sum of the degrees of the type‑30 vertices equals the number
of \(a\)-edges:

\[
\sum_{\text{type‑30}} \deg =E_a = m . \tag{5}
\]

A type‑75 vertex is incident to two \(a\)-edges (one from each apex) and
to the \(b\)-edges of the base polygon; consequently

\[
\sum_{\text{type‑75}} \deg =E_a+2E_b = m+2\cdot\frac{m}{2}=2m .\tag{6}
\]

Let \(d_1,\dots ,d_A\) be the degrees of the type‑30 vertices and
\(e_1,\dots ,e_B\) those of the type‑75 vertices.  From (5)–(6),

\[
\begin{cases}
d_1+\dots +d_A=m, & 3\le d_i\le 11,\\[2mm]
e_1+\dots +e_B=2m, & 3\le e_j\le 4 .
\end{cases}
\tag{7}
\]

Hence  

\[
A\ge \frac{m}{11},\qquad B\ge\frac{2m}{4}= \frac{m}{2}. \tag{8}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### Euler’s formula

With \(F=m\) and \(E=\tfrac32m\) (by (2)), Euler’s relation
\(V-E+F=2\) gives

\[
V=A+B=\frac{m}{2}+2 .\tag{9}
\]

Combining (8) with (9),

\[
\frac{m}{11}+\frac{m}{2}\le A+B=\frac{m}{2}+2
\Longrightarrow  \frac{13}{22}m\le\frac{m}{2}+2
\Longrightarrow 2m\le44 .
\]

Thus  

\[
\boxed{m\le 22}. \tag{10}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Realising \(m=22\)

Equality in (10) forces all the inequalities used above to be equalities.
Hence

* \(A=\dfrac{m}{11}=2\); both type‑30 vertices have degree \(11\).
* \(B=\dfrac{m}{2}=11\); every type‑75 vertex has degree \(4\).

Consequently the polyhedron consists of two vertices (the “apices’’) each
joined to all eleven other vertices, and the eleven remaining vertices are
joined cyclically by the \(b\)-edges.  In other words, it is a **bipyramid
over an 11‑gon**.

Let the base be a regular 11‑gon of side length \(b\); its circum‑radius is  

\[
R=\frac{b}{2\sin(\pi/11)} .
\]

