so \(\theta=30^{\circ}\); the base angles are \((180^{\circ}-30^{\circ})/2=75^{\circ}\).
Thus every face of the bipyramid is congruent to the required triangle.
The polyhedron is convex because the two apices lie on opposite sides of
the convex base polygon.

Hence a convex polyhedron with **\(22\) faces** satisfying the hypothesis
exists.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Conclusion  

The combinatorial and geometric arguments give

\[
\boxed{\,m_{\max}=22\,}.
\]

The unique (up to similarity) extremal polyhedron is the bipyramid over a
regular \(11\)-gon, whose 22 faces are congruent triangles with angles
\(30^{\circ},75^{\circ},75^{\circ}\).

